lf-checkout is the issue. He believes shoplifting has grown since the Dunmere bus interchange opened beside the car park on 16 February. Door-counter footfall averaged 14,100 visits a week in Q1, 15,200 in Q2 and 15,900 in Q3. He cites recorded shoplifting incidents in the store log: 6 in Q1, 11 in Q2 and 15 in Q3, mostly razors, cosmetics and batteries. 'My team is logging more than they ever have. We need a guard on the door.' Asked whether staff theft could be involved, he said he trusts his team and pointed to the ethics-line case being closed.\n- Ethics-line case 26-0871 and asset protection follow-up (log): Received 21 Aug, anonymous caller: 'The night stocker at Dunmere [name redacted] keeps taking boxes out the back door after close. Saw it on the 12th and the 19th.' Follow-up by AP investigator R. Okafor, 2 Sep: Reviewed rear-door and dock CCTV for 12 and 19 August, 22:00–06:00. On both nights the named stocker made two trips through the rear door carrying flattened cardboard bales to the compactor, as listed on the closing checklist. No merchandise or sealed cartons seen leaving. Night supervisor confirms bale runs are assigned to this employee. Case closed: unsubstantiated.\n- Store 14 interim shrink report, Q1–Q3 FY2026 (financial-record): Shrink as % of department sales, Q1 / Q2 / Q3. Grocery 0.8 / 1.1 / 1.3. Fresh 1.6 / 2.0 / 2.4. Health & beauty 2.9 / 3.6 / 4.4. General merchandise 2.0 / 2.5 / 3.1. Store total 1.5 / 1.9 / 2.3. Each department's share of store sales stayed within one point across all three quarters: grocery 46%, fresh 24%, health & beauty 14%, general merchandise 16%. Footnote: Q1–Q3 figures are interim estimates extrapolated from rotating department cycle counts. The annual full physical count, carried out by the independent count contractor, is scheduled for 7 December.\n- Email from Bram Castellane, Regional Operations Manager, 6 Nov (manager-account): Re: December count, store 14. Given holiday staffing pressure, I propose we take store 14 out of the 7 December full physical count and roll forward the Q3 book figure for year-end. We already know where that store's number is heading, and the count costs about 140 staff hours plus contractor fees. The other 37 stores would count as planned. Please confirm by 20 November so the contractor can re-plan its schedule.\n\nBRIEF:\nStore 14's rising shrink is not yet shown to be a real rise in losses. The figures are interim estimates with an error margin of about ±0.6 points per quarter. Across the chain, about half of the stores that showed two consecutive rises (13 of 27) were not confirmed by the year-end count. Store 14's earlier full counts were 1.5–1.8%. The reported rise is 0.8 points over two quarters. The shoplifting log counts rose chain-wide after the new incident app launched on 1 April, so that rise is not specific to store 14. The ethics-line tip was checked on CCTV and the stocker was carrying cardboard bales, so suspending him would be unjustified. The department mix is stable, so a change in what the store sells does not explain the rise. Self-checkout and the bus interchange are both plausible but unproven. The two nearby stores without them were flat or falling, which is only weak, indirect evidence. I'm fairly confident in the next step. Keep store 14 in the 7 December full count and reject dropping it. Hold off on guards, pulling or staffing the self-checkout lanes, and any personnel action until the count result is compared with the interim figures. Then act on what the count shows. If the rise is confirmed, look at the department and product-class detail.\n","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QC3PRgHb4TaDc7FNDBEBz2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
